<p class="font-body-md text-body-md text-on-surface italic">
              "Ubayy ibn Ka'b (رضي الله عنه) narrated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked him: 'O Abu al-Mundhir, do you know which Ayah of the Book of Allāh with you is the greatest?' He replied: 'Allāh and His Messenger know best.' The Prophet repeated it, whereat Ubayy said: <em>«Allāhu lā ilāha illā Huwa al-Ḥayyu al-Qayyūm»</em>. The Prophet struck his chest gently and said: 'May knowledge be pleasant for you, O Abu al-Mundhir!'"
            </p>
</div>
<p>
            Furthermore, the negation of <strong class="text-primary font-semibold">«سِنَةٞ وَلَا نَوۡمٞ» (Sinatun wa lā Nawm)</strong> signifies absolute perfection. <em>Sinah</em> denotes the initial onset of slumber or languor that dulls the senses, while <em>Nawm</em> constitutes deep sleep. Neither touches the Lord of Majesty.
